  <h1>Projects</h1>

  <p>
    Four things, with honest notes on what each one actually was. Most were
    coursework. Coursework still counts, as long as I say so.
  </p>

  <h2>ACC Timebank</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · lead developer · Google Apps Script, Google Sheets, Python</p>

  <p>
    A time-banking system for the Alachua Community Collective, a mutual-aid
    group in Florida. Time banking means members trade hours of work instead of
    money — an hour of yard work earns an hour of someone else's help. Somebody
    has to keep the ledger, and that was the job.
  </p>

  <p>
    ACC asked for two tracks: a fast, cheap version that worked with what they
    already had, and a fuller custom build. I led the first one and did most of
    the requirements gathering across both.
  </p>

  <p>
    I started in AppSheet and abandoned it — it couldn't express the transaction
    logic ACC needed without fighting it. I rebuilt on Google Apps Script with a
    Google Sheet as the database. That sounds like a downgrade until you know
    that ACC's member intake already ran through a Google Form: same ecosystem,
    no migration, and nobody had to learn a new tool to keep using the thing
    they were already using.
  </p>

  <p>What I built into it:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Transaction logic for crediting and debiting hours between members</li>
    <li>Admin reporting</li>
    <li>
      A burnout-risk flag that surfaces members who are hoarding hours or giving
      away far more than they take, so an admin knows to check in on them
    </li>
    <li>
      A Python script to migrate the existing form responses into the new system
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <strong>Status:</strong> the capstone ended and ACC no longer runs it. The
    deployment still exists but sits behind a Google login, so there's no public
    demo to link, and there's no public repository. A fuller writeup with code
    excerpts is the next thing I'm adding here.
  </p>

  <h2>BRHS Cybersecurity Risk Assessment</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · co-author · NIST CSF 2.0, CIS Controls v8</p>

  <p>
    A consulting report for a fictional healthcare organization: find the
    weaknesses, rank them, and recommend hardening that a real budget could
    absorb. We worked against NIST's Cybersecurity Framework and CIS Controls
    v8 rather than inventing our own categories, which is most of what made the
    findings defensible.
  </p>

  <p>
    This was coursework, not a live engagement, and the organization was
    invented for the assignment. The part I'd stand behind is the method:
    mapping findings to a published framework so a reader can check the work.
  </p>

  <h2>Capsim Business Simulation</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · strategic management</p>

  <p>
    A multi-round business simulation where a team runs a company against
    competitors. We ran a niche differentiation strategy aimed at the high-tech
    segments, making pricing, R&amp;D, marketing, and production calls each
    round and living with them the next one.
  </p>

  <p>
    Worth including for one reason: it's the closest thing in my coursework to
    making decisions with incomplete information and a cost attached to being
    wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>This site</h2>
  <p class="meta">2026 · ongoing · Astro, Cloudflare Pages, GitHub Actions</p>

  <p>
    The one you can actually inspect. Static site on Astro, hosted on Cloudflare
    Pages, DNS and TLS managed by me, built by GitHub Actions on every push,
    with a branch rule that stops anything reaching production unless the build
    passes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The repository is public and includes a decision log explaining every choice
    in it, including the reversals. Terraform for the Cloudflare configuration
    and a small Cloudflare Worker are the next pieces.
  </p>

